lojbab's and others' comments about languages being finished only when
they're dead seem irrelevant to the intent of the one saying "finished". All
natlangs are finished. That doesn't mean they're not changing. It means
they're adequate for expressing anything the speaker of the native culture
of that language wants to express. Esperanto is finished, using this
definition. Lojban is not, because it doesn't have the vocabulary necessary
to express everything that a native speaker might want to express. Lojban is
nearer to being finished than Loglan ever was, and what it needs to become
finished is simply for people to use it (a lot).

stevo

